WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.340
Anticipation!

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:04.400
Now you're playing with Brain Power!

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:27.180
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:28.100
I'm Mike.

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:29.500
I'm Sean. And I'm Joe.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:36.160
And guys, today we have what Rare and Mattel are calling Nintendo's first video board game.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.280
I guess it sort of is that.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:39.720
Is this correct, Mike?

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:41.440
Like, do you agree?

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:43.140
Do your eyes lie to you?

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:49.300
Well, you know, it's semantics here, but it's not really Nintendo's first video board game.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:53.320
In fact, Nintendo's first video board game is probably like Fortune Street.

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:56.780
You know, they didn't have a hand in the development of this game.

00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:02.180
And I don't appreciate them also stealing Nintendo's tagline of the now you're playing with power.

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:06.880
And it just seems like Mattel really wants you to believe that Nintendo made this and not Rare.

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:08.900
Really getting off on the wrong foot here.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:11.500
Yeah, I just... I guess they could be saying, like, well, it's...

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:14.360
I guess, like, a lot of people called the machine the Nintendo.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.000
So it's, like, the first one available on the Nintendo.

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:18.240
That makes sense to me.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:19.880
Okay, I like that. But here's the thing.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.600
When you see video board game, right, are you thinking, like, Pictionary?

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:26.420
Or would you be expecting Monopoly?

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:31.560
Yeah, I'd be expecting more of a board game than just, like, a little mini game that has a board involved.

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:33.420
I mean, there's a little more rules, too.

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:34.740
But, yeah, I'm expecting Monopoly.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:40.940
Well, Anticipation says if you're bored with board games and you're tired of pursuing trivia,

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:43.620
which I guess is just a reference to Trivia Pursuit,

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:49.120
if you're bored of both of those things and you've had your fill of ethical questions,

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:51.300
then you're ready for Anticipation.

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:53.100
What the hell does that mean? What does that mean?

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:56.920
I don't know. I guess there's no ethics involved in Anticipation.

00:01:58.340 --> 00:01:59.620
I feel like that.

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:05.300
Did, like, the manual writer just, like, he had, like, a Freudian slip there

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:11.340
and, like, he's always just grilled on his politics or something whenever he plays both games with people?

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:13.300
It was, like, a passive-aggressive, like, shot at his manager.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.580
He thought his manager would read it before he printed it.

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:17.720
Right. It's, like, had enough of your neighbor?

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:21.300
You're ready for Anticipation.

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:24.240
Is Jim pissing you off again?

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:30.840
Yeah. I like the idea, though, that you're bored with board games and now you're going to play this

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:34.760
and because it's a video board game instead, it's going to, like, change everything.

00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:36.280
No cleanup, no nothing.

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:39.140
Everybody hates the cleanup.

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:40.900
It's all fun and games setting it up.

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:42.040
Some people don't even play it.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:42.960
They just like to set it up.

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:46.600
I don't know if you guys ever played the board game Mousetrap, but...

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:48.640
Oh, yeah. I would only have set that thing up.

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:49.620
I never used it.

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:51.140
Yeah, I've never played it.

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:52.500
I've just set it up and...

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:53.540
Yeah, the first time...

00:02:53.540 --> 00:03:00.600
Set it off. The first time me and my mom played Mousetrap, one of the contraptions broke, so it was just useless.

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:03.720
Well, that stinks, and there's a lot of pieces, so...

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:05.140
There are. Did you return it?

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:07.120
No. Okay. That's also...

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:11.100
It's not too late. So you're just, like, casually revealing that you're rich to the rest of the audience.

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:14.060
You're, like, getting board games, they're broken, and you're like, this is fine.

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:21.360
Uh, I'm just saying that, you know, I was, like, seven, and my mom probably had more important things to worry about, so...

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:23.640
Actually, no, it's just child neglect.

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:52.480
Got it. All right, well, I mean, let's talk about anticipation as the best we can, because it's just basically Pictionary, like I said, except for there are dots on the screen that appear for different categories, and then slowly the computer fills and connects those dots, and then I guess whoever's the first person to kind of understand the image that the computer is drawing buzzes in and then tries to guess the answer.

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:53.960
Anything else you guys want to add?

00:03:54.160 --> 00:04:06.440
Well, I think the important distinction you made there between this and Pictionary is that the computer is drawing it, and I think that a big draw of, like, actual Pictionary is that a friend of yours is drawing something.

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:11.040
Like, that takes away already, like, I think, a major part of, like, the appeal to something like this.

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:18.860
Well, not even just that, but, like, I always liked to be the one to draw, and you don't get to do that in this game.

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:33.500
Right. Like, you are just, there's just this fifth, if you're playing in a four-player game, there's just this fifth, like, embodiment of something that is drawing for you, and it just, it feels like half a game even there.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:44.540
We didn't get into, like, the board game on top of this yet, but that's already, like, when I saw the screen caps for this, I'm like, oh, I'll get to draw something?

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:48.560
Like, is this, like, I actually get to put pixels down on the screen?

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:51.680
And then when I found out that I couldn't, I was very disappointed.

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:56.840
Yeah, you'll likely have to wait for Color a Dinosaur before you're able to draw anything on the NES.

00:04:56.840 --> 00:05:02.100
You know that my anticipation is high for Color a Dinosaur.

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:10.880
So, before we talk about the larger board game, which I feel is the second part of the gameplay, not as important as this first element.

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:16.360
You know, it is a bummer that you can't draw, but obviously that's because you're not divided into teams.

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:19.100
Each of you is playing for your own win.

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:31.360
And so, there's this whole element of, like, the game itself just being based around connect the dots and figuring out fastest what the object is.

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:36.480
I didn't find too many objects to be impossible to guess within a few seconds.

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:38.560
Well, were you only playing on easy, Mike?

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:43.580
I don't know if I played on the hard mode, but I played on the whatever the other difficult...

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:44.940
Yeah, medium, if that's what it was called.

00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:48.240
Okay. Well, I mean, I'll have some stories later about hard mode, but...

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:53.160
Which one gotcha? Which I'd be interested to hear, like, one that you were just like, I would have never guessed that.

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:56.500
Okay. Well, this is, like, several stories.

00:05:56.900 --> 00:05:59.040
Okay, good. We need a story time.

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:10.880
Okay. So, I'll first preface this by saying that in the higher difficulties, and I think later in, like, just the normal game, the dots go away.

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:15.580
So, you can't sort of draw them in your mind before the computer gets there.

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:23.000
Um, but when you get to hard mode, it has these weird, like, there's a disconnect between...

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:31.480
Sometimes it'll give you a category that's supposed to give you a clue as to what it might be, and it is completely different than what it is drawing.

00:06:31.480 --> 00:06:37.620
Like, I guess anything that, like, has one or two moving parts is science.

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:39.580
Um, and...

00:06:39.580 --> 00:06:45.080
But in hard mode, if you're playing a computer, their reaction time gets much, much greater.

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:59.420
So, in one instance, uh, the computer had drawn maybe three or four very small lines, and the computer guesses and immediately types in, like, uh, oil can.

00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:01.100
Or not, uh, no, blowtorch.

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:02.020
It was blowtorch.

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:13.320
And then it's correct, and then the computer spends another five to six seconds drawing this very intricate blowtorch that the computer player guessed in three seconds.

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:19.860
So, there's also this balance of, like, I guess, where do they draw the line of what's guessable or not?

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:23.400
So, your competition gets a lot stronger as you get...

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:27.320
And the items get a bit more complex as you go higher in difficulty.

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:30.480
And now, I don't know about you, but I am...

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:39.800
I'm not interested in my reaction times versus a computer, and that's why I wasn't too interested in playing the harder difficulties of this game,

00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:48.140
because I feel like I got the full experience on Medium, and it is interesting to hear that the dots go away and that the category titles go away,

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:51.940
so you don't know exactly what type of thing they're drawing for which category.

00:07:52.300 --> 00:08:01.320
But I think the idea of, well, the way that it gets harder is that the computer gets smarter and it chimes in faster than you would recognize what it is,

00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:03.160
is kind of just like a...

00:08:03.160 --> 00:08:08.220
Like, maybe that works out better if you have four human players, but when a computer is added to the mix,

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:15.980
even if it's three regular people and a computer, I feel like the computer has an obvious advantage there that I'm not interested in checking out.

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:18.140
Yeah. I mean, we kind of had this problem with Jeopardy!

00:08:18.260 --> 00:08:22.700
to an extent, too, but it wasn't as, like, obvious and as problematic,

00:08:22.700 --> 00:08:28.260
because, like, it's just a question, and people either know or don't know that,

00:08:28.280 --> 00:08:36.340
but if you're just drawing these abstract lines and trying to form pictures out of them when they're incomplete,

00:08:36.620 --> 00:08:42.420
then it gets a little bit more ridiculous that a computer can guess out of three lines or something along those lines.

00:08:42.520 --> 00:08:43.980
Sean, this might... Yeah. Oh, go ahead, Joe.

00:08:44.180 --> 00:08:45.400
No, I was going to say, I didn't...

00:08:45.400 --> 00:08:49.520
Because I only played Medium as well, and I had a similar thought where I was like,

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:53.040
I should play hard, but, like, I feel like they're just going to make this game longer.

00:08:53.320 --> 00:08:56.700
Like, there would just be more turns where the computer reacts to be more quickly,

00:08:56.700 --> 00:08:59.020
and then I have to just wait through their turns longer, et cetera.

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:03.720
But, you know, I'm of two minds of another thing that they do to make it more difficult,

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:07.140
at least as Medium, as you get to the end of the Medium board,

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:13.040
where they take away the spaces that they put at the bottom to tell you how many letters are in the word.

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:16.520
And at first I thought, okay, that's a little more organic,

00:09:16.520 --> 00:09:17.880
where I feel like, oh, it's just more...

00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:21.280
it's harder for me, and it's not because I'm competing against a computer that already knows the answer.

00:09:21.460 --> 00:09:23.660
But then after a while, there were some things...

00:09:23.660 --> 00:09:25.040
There was one that was like...

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:27.200
It was like a... just like a stick figure.

00:09:27.380 --> 00:09:28.960
And I was like, well, that could be person.

00:09:29.140 --> 00:09:30.680
It could be man.

00:09:30.860 --> 00:09:32.800
It could be... you know, it was man.

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:38.220
And it's like the amount of spaces that would actually give you that clue as to what it actually was.

00:09:38.460 --> 00:09:40.120
Exactly, yeah. There were like a...

00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:43.940
And there were a couple of those where there was like, well, this could just be called a lot of different things,

00:09:43.940 --> 00:09:48.500
or I could word this a lot of different ways, and without those spaces, there's no leeway on that.

00:09:48.620 --> 00:09:54.080
I had one as well, and it was a picture of a barbecue with like smoke coming out of it.

00:09:54.420 --> 00:09:57.080
But I mean, I only say that now because that was the answer.

00:09:57.220 --> 00:09:58.100
I just thought it was a grill.

00:09:58.340 --> 00:10:02.060
So I typed in grill, and it was wrong, or I typed in G.

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:05.640
But I don't know.

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:07.900
Well, yeah, there's a lot of weird things there.

00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:17.080
But just going back to like easy mode, there's a huge discrepancy in the difficulty of the different modes.

00:10:17.080 --> 00:10:20.820
Because in easy mode, I finished the game by myself.

00:10:20.840 --> 00:10:25.460
No other player just even interacted with the board at all.

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:26.680
I just answered them all.

00:10:26.680 --> 00:10:32.960
And once you go to, I don't know what it's like in Medium, but with Hard, they just start guessing randomly.

00:10:34.060 --> 00:10:37.980
It's just like there's absolutely no difficulty curve there.

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:45.520
It's just a lot. Yeah, it sounds like there's a big jump because on Medium, I beat the game, and maybe I was playing with...

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:49.720
There were three players in my game, and like each other player maybe answered once or maybe twice.

00:10:50.140 --> 00:10:52.400
Yeah. And that was just because I didn't...

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:56.040
There was one thing that was a beehive, but it just...

00:10:56.040 --> 00:10:57.040
I mean, maybe I have...

00:10:57.040 --> 00:10:57.920
Do you know what I'm talking about?

00:10:57.960 --> 00:10:58.880
I saw that one, yeah.

00:10:59.020 --> 00:11:02.700
Maybe I have not seen enough beehives in my life, but this was not...

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:03.720
There's not like a beehive.

00:11:03.780 --> 00:11:05.420
It's like a man-made construction.

00:11:05.420 --> 00:11:06.520
It's like an industrial beehive, yeah.

00:11:06.640 --> 00:11:09.280
Yeah. So I never would have gotten that.

00:11:09.700 --> 00:11:15.740
An interesting problem that continues to occur, too, in these rare-developed game show-like games

00:11:15.740 --> 00:11:23.220
is that they have no perceivable way of giving the computer a wrong answer in a different word.

00:11:23.520 --> 00:11:29.320
Yeah. They just kind of misspell or add like symbols to the words.

00:11:29.920 --> 00:11:36.180
And in this game, that really hurts it because you see something like, let's say it's Volcano,

00:11:36.640 --> 00:11:43.420
and they'll get like V-O-L star symbol A percentage sign, you know?

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:49.000
And it's like, okay, well, that's a pretty good clue for me now, too, of what this actually is.

00:11:49.180 --> 00:11:54.060
I thought it was weird that they did that because I remember it was a Wheel of Fortune that had that

00:11:54.060 --> 00:11:58.420
where I remember thinking, well, okay, because they're making a guess and the computer can't come up

00:11:58.420 --> 00:12:00.860
with a realistic guess that a person would make based on this.

00:12:00.860 --> 00:12:06.200
So it's just giving you part of the answer as like a compromise to like make it as close to real as they can.

00:12:06.400 --> 00:12:10.480
But for this, the way that you answer is you go through and you select each letter.

00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:15.200
And once you select one letter incorrectly, it says you're wrong.

00:12:15.400 --> 00:12:20.920
Yeah. So I thought, why didn't the computer just select one wrong letter and tell it it's wrong?

00:12:21.020 --> 00:12:24.620
And then it doesn't have to have that kind of game-breaking thing where it's like, well,

00:12:24.700 --> 00:12:26.640
this is what would happen if another human guessed also.

00:12:26.780 --> 00:12:27.760
It's exactly what you would see.

00:12:27.880 --> 00:12:29.840
And then it doesn't have to give away the whole answer.

00:12:29.840 --> 00:12:31.680
Wow. I didn't even think of that.

00:12:31.860 --> 00:12:33.440
This is, yeah, that's very strange.

00:12:33.660 --> 00:12:38.680
Yeah. That's what I actually expected to happen too, because to be honest, I like the fact that

00:12:38.680 --> 00:12:44.620
on the player side, it doesn't waste your time if you don't know what it is off the bat.

00:12:44.720 --> 00:12:49.340
You know, it doesn't make you type in every single letter and then be like, oh, actually

00:12:49.340 --> 00:12:50.400
we weren't looking for grill.

00:12:50.540 --> 00:12:52.820
We were looking for barbecue, you know, like to Sean's point.

00:12:52.820 --> 00:12:58.200
So I think that the fact that the computer is then allowed to like just draw the whole

00:12:58.200 --> 00:13:02.860
thing and write out the whole thing is a weird tell.

00:13:03.180 --> 00:13:05.540
Yeah. To help the players.

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:08.060
Just for the actual players.

00:13:08.180 --> 00:13:09.080
Yeah. That's very strange.

00:13:09.080 --> 00:13:13.040
I guess we should have found a way to play this together.

00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:19.480
I don't think that it would change our critical review of this game, but maybe our experience

00:13:19.480 --> 00:13:23.800
would have been a bit more in line with what people in 88 may have taught.

00:13:23.800 --> 00:13:30.800
Well, you know, I did play it with my fiance and I think that the thing here is that, you

00:13:30.800 --> 00:13:35.900
know, unless you have the NES four score, which was a add on accessory that would let you play

00:13:35.900 --> 00:13:36.860
with four controllers.

00:13:37.160 --> 00:13:38.480
It's just called a multi-tap.

00:13:38.820 --> 00:13:43.200
Yeah. They called it the four score, but that's funny.

00:13:43.480 --> 00:13:48.560
It's true. Like multi-tap is now like the official word across all systems for those things.

00:13:48.560 --> 00:13:52.160
But like, I guess at the time, Nintendo was like, we have to make this exciting.

00:13:52.160 --> 00:13:58.320
Uh, anyway, you know, as long as there's one computer in the mix, you're technically

00:13:58.320 --> 00:14:02.700
always playing the same kind of game that you would play with or without another person.

00:14:02.700 --> 00:14:08.060
It's just that on the easier difficulties, easier difficulties, it gets to be more competitive

00:14:08.060 --> 00:14:09.180
with the human players.

00:14:09.520 --> 00:14:14.160
Whereas in the later difficulties, they're just kind of at the mercy of the response time

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:15.380
that the computer chooses.

00:14:16.340 --> 00:14:17.460
Yeah. The drawings though.

00:14:17.460 --> 00:14:21.900
I mean, you know, I'm sure there were some funny ones, but for the most part,

00:14:21.900 --> 00:14:25.420
the finished drawings are pretty competent line art.

00:14:26.320 --> 00:14:30.980
Yeah. I was surprised that there were as much as that there were definitely plenty that I was

00:14:30.980 --> 00:14:32.880
like, well, that's ridiculous. That's not what that looks like.

00:14:33.320 --> 00:14:37.520
Um, that most of them, I was like, well, even, even with this like NES thing, even like

00:14:37.520 --> 00:14:40.820
a quarter of the way through the drawing, I do have a good idea.

00:14:40.920 --> 00:14:44.640
I mean, they, they did a decent job of making sure they didn't do any, they didn't do too

00:14:44.640 --> 00:14:48.020
many things that were like impossible to get like halfway through the drawing.

00:14:48.020 --> 00:14:51.940
Uh, granted, I'm looking at one right now because I'm watching a video as we're speaking that

00:14:51.940 --> 00:14:56.500
I've probably never would have gotten, but, uh, but for the most part that they, they did

00:14:56.500 --> 00:14:57.340
better than I expected.

00:14:57.520 --> 00:15:04.160
They did about as well as, uh, the, the general populace would, would do in a Pictionary game,

00:15:04.320 --> 00:15:05.260
I'd say. That's a good point.

00:15:05.380 --> 00:15:12.640
Stick figures and, uh, uh, bad, like poor angles, bad perception or bad perspective, you

00:15:12.640 --> 00:15:17.080
know. Yeah. It would, it would be something different if there was actually like good graphics behind

00:15:17.080 --> 00:15:23.580
all of this, you know, it is kind of, I just posted one in the chat of a crab and I think

00:15:23.580 --> 00:15:27.980
like, you know, that's a, that's better than I would draw of a crab, but it's also not a

00:15:27.980 --> 00:15:29.140
great drawing of a crab.

00:15:29.220 --> 00:15:31.680
Yeah. It's like the art typical crab.

00:15:32.040 --> 00:15:37.460
Yeah. I mean, you could even, uh, argue that that's like Totoro instead of a crab, if you look at

00:15:37.460 --> 00:15:39.040
it the right way. Oh, I see it now.

00:15:39.200 --> 00:15:40.500
Yeah. Mm-hmm. Thank you.

00:15:40.500 --> 00:15:43.360
Or, um, Adelibird from the Pokemon.

00:15:43.700 --> 00:15:46.800
